@@@ mail-dot-mara-1012
type: email
title: Please read this before anything else
author: dotmatrix
date: 1999-10-12T22:31:00-07:00
path: /mail/keeper/1999-10-12_please-read.eml
role: critical
---
From: Dot Okafor <dot@beacon.lighthouse.net>
To: Mara Quinlan <keeper@beacon.lighthouse.net>
Cc: Ray Tillman <ray@beacon.lighthouse.net>
Date: Tue, 12 Oct 1999 22:31:57 -0700
Subject: Please read this before anything else

Mara,

I'm sorry to be the one sending this. Eli sent me his "raw" file
yesterday. I've been through it and the posted one.

1. There is 60 Hz hum, with harmonics at 120 and 180, under the
   voice and only under the voice. The noise before and after it
   has none. That's two recordings joined together. A splice.
2. Cape Sorrow has had no mains power since 1987. A voice coming
   from there cannot carry wall-socket hum.
3. The voice has slow reel-to-reel wow, about 0.5 Hz. The noise
   around it has cassette flutter. Two different machines.
4. The raw file still has its comment field: "dub from reel -
   box 3 side B". Nobody types that about a radio signal.

I think the voice is an old tape, played on a reel deck and mixed
into a noise recording. I ▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒
▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒ who would have a tape like that ▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒

I haven't told anyone else. I'm going to ask Henrik about the
radio procedure, quietly, because he'll know. Please don't let
the newspaper run this.

Dot

@@@ mail-dot-henrik-1013
type: email
title: a question about old procedure
author: dotmatrix
date: 1999-10-13T09:10:00-07:00
path: /mail/dotmatrix/1999-10-13_to-henrik.eml
role: critical
---
From: Dot Okafor <dot@beacon.lighthouse.net>
To: Henrik Vale <hvale@newportnet.com>
Date: Wed, 13 Oct 1999 09:10:44 -0700
Subject: a question about old procedure

Henrik,

I know you left and I don't blame you. I need your ears for one
thing, privately. In CS-0903, "Cape Sorrow radio, the light is out,
I say again" and then two numbers - does that sound to you like a
real 1999 transmission, or like something older?

You can read the thread I'm about to post if you still have a
login. Please don't post in it.

Dot

@@@ mail-henrik-dot-1014
type: email
title: Re: a question about old procedure
author: hvale
date: 1999-10-14T07:55:00-07:00
path: /mail/dotmatrix/1999-10-14_from-henrik.eml
role: critical
---
From: Henrik Vale <hvale@newportnet.com>
To: Dot Okafor <dot@beacon.lighthouse.net>
Date: Thu, 14 Oct 1999 07:55:20 -0700
Subject: Re: a question about old procedure

Dot,

Older. That's a Coast Guard communications drill, the way they ran
them in the late seventies: station name three times, the drill
phrase, then the drill number. "Four seven" would be drill 47.
Nobody has run a drill like that on 2182 since the stations
were consolidated.

Whoever made that recording knew the old procedures very well, or
had a tape of them. I said in September it stank. I'm sorry I
was right.

I'll look at your thread. I won't post.

Henrik

